    ├── Pitfalls   : httpx.Client vs requests.Session have different constructor defaults (e.g. timeout handling: httpx requires explicit timeout config to 
    │   avoid None=no-timeout behavior differences); Exception types differ: requests raises requests.exceptions.HTTPError, httpx raises httpx.HTTPStatusError 
    │   — error handling code calling raise_for_status() would need adjusted except clauses upstream; httpx.Client must be closed/used as context manager for 
    │   connection pooling parity with requests.Session
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── The code uses requests.Session() to create a reusable session object, then calls .get() with a timeout, .raise_for_status(), and .json() on the 
        response — all synchronous, blocking calls. httpx offers an httpx.Client with an equivalent synchronous API surface (get, raise_for_status, json) that 
        could substitute with light glue code, matching the docstring's stated substitution target.

